    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d946">

                <pb facs="0788" n="1988" xml:id="pg1988"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">663.001/12–254: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 946<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France (Dillon) to the Department of State</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d946-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret<lb/>niact</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-12-02T12:00:00+01:00">December 2,
                        1954—noon</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2306. Re Department telegram 2008.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d946-f2"/> I was unable
                    to see Mendès prior to his appearance before Foreign
                    Affairs Committee which commenced at 10:30 this morning. I saw
                        Soutou<anchor n="3" xml:id="d946-f3"/> instead.
                        Soutou said Mendès felt strongly
                    some <hi rendition="#italic">démarche</hi> necessary, but he was very pleased
                    with Secretary’s suggestion contained in last sentence of reference telegram and
                    said he thought French Government would much prefer <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >démarche</hi> to be tripartite. He said their only reason for suggesting
                    unilateral approach was that they had not realized that we might be prepared to
                    join them in tripartite approach on this subject. Soutou
                    said that he was accompanying Mendès to Foreign Affairs
                    Committee meeting and would deliver to him your message. I then informed
                        Jebb of what had occurred,
                        Jebb having previously told me that
                    being without instructions, he could not join in any <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >démarche</hi> to Mendès-France this morning. Jebb thought suggestion for tripartite <hi
                        rendition="#italic">démarche</hi> excellent, and is making strong telephonic
                    recommendation to Foreign Office in London that British Government promptly
                    accept this suggestion.</p>
                <p>On my return to Embassy, I received phone call from Seydoux who said Mendès-France fully
                    accepted suggestion for tripartite approach. Jebb will contact Quai d’Orsay as soon as he receives answer
                    from London, and if answer is favorable, French expect propose first meeting be
                    held in London this afternoon to discuss form and substance of tripartite <hi
                        rendition="#italic">démarche</hi>.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Dillon</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d946-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d946-f1-content">
                <p>Repeated to Moscow, Vienna, Bonn, and London.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d946-f2" n="2" xml:id="d946-f2-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d945-f6">footnote 6, <hi rendition="#italic"
                    >supra</hi></ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d946-f3" n="3" xml:id="d946-f3-content">
                <p>Jean Soutou, Assistant Director of the Cabinet of the
                    Ministry of Foreign Affairs after June 1954.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
